\section{Western hyphenation}

Typographical hyphenation separates polysyllabic words at permissible syllable
boundaries, letting the paragraph breaker distribute the remaining width more
evenly across justified lines. Without such internal breakpoints, an unusually
long word like internationalization would migrate to the following line and
leave a conspicuously overstretched predecessor behind it.

Compound expressions such as well-known already provide a breakpoint immediately
after the existing hyphen, so the hyphenation pass supplements rather than
replaces the segmentation supplied by Unicode line breaking.
